1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
He introduced the lowly peanut to big business and changed the course of southern agriculture.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:14,000
He turned soybeans into plastic and carved his place in history.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
An orphan slave set free with a microscope and a vision.

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, George Washington Carver Tech on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
What do biodiesel fuel and peanut butter have in common?

6
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:45,240
How are crayons and space travel connected?

7
00:00:45,241 --> 00:00:48,960
What are soybeans doing in our automobiles?

8
00:00:48,961 --> 00:00:52,760
These marvels can all be traced back to the inventive mind of a

9
00:00:52,761 --> 00:00:57,760
man who was born a slave, but emerged a master of scientific

10
00:00:57,761 --> 00:00:58,800
discovery.

11
00:00:58,801 --> 00:01:03,800
He was able to just look around him and see the raw materials

12
00:01:03,801 --> 00:01:06,800
and then see the human need that was out there

13
00:01:06,801 --> 00:01:08,800
and bring the two together.

14
00:01:08,801 --> 00:01:10,800
And so my own personal view of Carver

15
00:01:10,801 --> 00:01:13,800
is that he was a master integrator.

16
00:01:13,801 --> 00:01:16,800
Carver had the most remarkable heart,

17
00:01:16,801 --> 00:01:19,800
and that's the thing I think I love the most about his life.

18
00:01:19,801 --> 00:01:22,800
I mean, as tremendous of a scientist as he was,

19
00:01:22,801 --> 00:01:25,800
coming out with so many useful products

20
00:01:25,801 --> 00:01:28,800
that even today, I don't even know what we would do

21
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:34,300
without, his heart was the best. Dr. George Washington Carver

22
00:01:34,301 --> 00:01:37,240
is well known throughout the world for his revolutionary

23
00:01:37,241 --> 00:01:41,740
research, particularly with a peanut.

24
00:01:41,741 --> 00:01:45,140
In the early 1900s, he discovered and developed more

25
00:01:45,141 --> 00:01:49,960
than 300 new uses for the legume in food products and industrial

26
00:01:49,961 --> 00:01:54,560
applications, from face cream to a cure for dandruff, from

27
00:01:54,561 --> 00:01:58,260
from linoleum flooring to motor oil.

28
00:01:58,261 --> 00:02:01,800
Carver saw infinite possibilities in the peanut plant.

29
00:02:01,801 --> 00:02:05,700
He literally inspired other researchers

30
00:02:05,701 --> 00:02:10,700
all across this country to take a deeper look at this plant.

31
00:02:10,701 --> 00:02:14,920
And of course, I believe that the end of the line

32
00:02:14,921 --> 00:02:19,560
has not been reached on what he uncovered.

33
00:02:19,561 --> 00:02:22,360
But Carver's legacy goes far beyond his accomplishments

34
00:02:22,361 --> 00:02:26,460
with what was called at the time the lowly goober.

35
00:02:26,461 --> 00:02:29,760
He toiled for more than 40 years in his laboratory at Tuskegee

36
00:02:29,761 --> 00:02:33,600
Institute in Alabama and is credited with countless

37
00:02:33,601 --> 00:02:36,600
breakthroughs in agricultural research.

38
00:02:36,601 --> 00:02:40,920
The impact of that was just astonishing.

39
00:02:40,921 --> 00:02:45,020
Carver, through the kind of research he did, was able to

40
00:02:45,021 --> 00:02:48,820
really lead the revitalization, really, of a whole region,

41
00:02:48,821 --> 00:02:51,920
the southern region. He really is one of the

42
00:02:51,921 --> 00:02:54,320
American heroes. If there's anyone you're going to learn

43
00:02:54,321 --> 00:02:57,120
about, you should learn about, Carver would be one of those

44
00:02:57,121 --> 00:03:01,120
people. George Carver was born into

45
00:03:01,121 --> 00:03:07,120
slavery in 1864 in Diamond Grove, Missouri.

46
00:03:07,121 --> 00:03:11,520
He was the property of Moses Carver.

47
00:03:11,521 --> 00:03:14,320
Towards the end of the Civil War, southern bushwhackers raided the

48
00:03:14,321 --> 00:03:18,960
Carver's farm and kidnapped baby George and his mother.

49
00:03:18,961 --> 00:03:22,660
Moses Carver hired a Union scout named John Bentley

50
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:26,260
to trace after them and try to recover his property.

51
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:29,200
Bentley was able to find baby George,

52
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:32,700
and the mother Mary was never seen again.

53
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,100
We do not know what happened.

54
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:37,720
George was near death when he was found.

55
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:39,420
Moses and his wife took care of him

56
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:43,220
like he was their own child.

57
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:45,460
Too frail to work in the fields,

58
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:48,360
he helped out with the household chores.

59
00:03:48,361 --> 00:03:52,200
George was inspired to paint after visiting a neighbor's home

60
00:03:52,201 --> 00:03:54,860
and seeing works of art for the first time.

61
00:03:54,861 --> 00:03:58,600
So he came back, created his own brushes with twigs,

62
00:03:58,601 --> 00:04:02,000
and he would take nature's berries and grasses

63
00:04:02,001 --> 00:04:08,620
and create colors, an array of colors, and begin to paint.

64
00:04:08,621 --> 00:04:10,320
Young George Carver's inventiveness

65
00:04:10,321 --> 00:04:12,660
and hunger for learning compelled him to seek

66
00:04:12,661 --> 00:04:17,720
what was impossible for many African Americans of his day,

67
00:04:17,721 --> 00:04:23,720
education. Around the age of 11, he left

68
00:04:23,721 --> 00:04:27,720
the Carver Farm and walked 10 miles to Neosho, Missouri,

69
00:04:27,721 --> 00:04:30,720
determined to attend classes at the Lincoln School for Negro

70
00:04:30,721 --> 00:04:37,720
Children. He worked for his room and board

71
00:04:37,721 --> 00:04:41,720
at the home of Mariah and Andrew Watkins, who lived next door to

72
00:04:41,721 --> 00:04:45,720
the school. The story is that at lunch hour,

73
00:04:45,721 --> 00:04:48,560
he would jump the fence between the school and Aunt Mariah's

74
00:04:48,660 --> 00:04:52,560
house and go over and help her with her laundry.

75
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:54,560
Many years later, George was inspired

76
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:59,660
to draw this picture of the school and the Watkins house.

77
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:03,180
George studied hard and got his high school diploma.

78
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:06,680
He applied to Highland College in Kansas and was accepted.

79
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:08,680
It was a big moment.

80
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:11,480
Then he went to Highland, and he showed up,

81
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:14,180
and they said, we didn't know you were black.

82
00:05:14,181 --> 00:05:20,180
and they turned him away. It was a huge disappointment.

83
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:22,920
George didn't give up. In his mid-20s,

84
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:25,020
he applied to Simpson College in Iowa,

85
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:28,420
where blacks were allowed. He was accepted

86
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:30,940
and began taking classes in painting.

87
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:34,140
His first love was art, and he had a teacher

88
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:37,240
whose name was Etta Budd. She was very dubious

89
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:40,680
about whether a black could make it in art.

90
00:05:40,681 --> 00:05:45,480
It was a near impossibility. And she was a little concerned

91
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:49,480
about him being... He was not the first black at Simpson, but

92
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:53,220
he was the only one at the time. Carver's complex

93
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:56,020
understanding of nature came through in his paintings and

94
00:05:56,120 --> 00:06:00,540
drawings at Simpson. Etta Budd noticed this talent

95
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:03,940
and encouraged him to change his course of study to horticulture.

96
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:09,480
Carver took her advice and transferred to the Iowa State

97
00:06:09,481 --> 00:06:13,620
College of Agriculture in Ames, where he became the first black

98
00:06:13,621 --> 00:06:16,720
student. And he had some trouble with

99
00:06:16,721 --> 00:06:20,220
dining room privileges and other things.

100
00:06:20,221 --> 00:06:25,320
But he overcame them by his knowledge, which was so

101
00:06:25,321 --> 00:06:30,440
conspicuously immense that you didn't have to be in his

102
00:06:30,441 --> 00:06:37,140
presence very long to realize that this man knew things.

103
00:06:37,141 --> 00:06:40,180
When Carver completed his bachelor's degree, Iowa state

104
00:06:40,181 --> 00:06:44,040
officials offered him a teaching position.

105
00:06:44,041 --> 00:06:47,680
The first black student in the college's history was now its

106
00:06:47,681 --> 00:06:51,080
first African-American professor.

107
00:06:51,081 --> 00:06:56,200
Though Carver was content at Iowa State, in April 1896, he was

108
00:06:56,201 --> 00:07:01,900
offered an opportunity that would change his life forever.

109
00:07:01,901 --> 00:07:05,740
Booker T. Washington, a leader among the newly freed slave

110
00:07:05,741 --> 00:07:09,180
population in the South, invited him to come to Tuskegee

111
00:07:09,181 --> 00:07:12,540
Institute, an all-black college in Alabama.

112
00:07:12,541 --> 00:07:15,780
When Booker T. became aware of Carver that there was an

113
00:07:15,781 --> 00:07:19,880
agricultural expert in the country anywhere who was black,

114
00:07:19,881 --> 00:07:22,900
that was huge. He was really the only one in the whole

115
00:07:22,901 --> 00:07:28,200
country. Booker T. badly wanted someone like that.

116
00:07:28,201 --> 00:07:32,140
At the age of 31, George Washington Carver had found his

117
00:07:32,141 --> 00:07:36,380
true calling. He went to Alabama with a mission,

118
00:07:36,381 --> 00:07:39,880
to help a once enslaved population find its way to

119
00:07:39,881 --> 00:07:43,880
self-sufficiency. Little did he know what landmark

120
00:07:43,881 --> 00:07:51,300
discoveries lay ahead. We now return to George

121
00:07:51,301 --> 00:07:56,940
Washington Carver Tech on Modern Marvels.

122
00:07:56,941 --> 00:08:01,540
Today, 50,000 peanut farms thrive in the United States.

123
00:08:01,541 --> 00:08:06,180
most of them in the South. Virtually all of them have taken

124
00:08:06,181 --> 00:08:10,900
root in the past century, thanks to the horticulture professor who

125
00:08:10,901 --> 00:08:17,780
ventured to Tuskegee with a sense of mission in 1896.

126
00:08:17,781 --> 00:08:21,780
When George Washington Carver arrived by rail in Alabama, he saw

127
00:08:21,781 --> 00:08:27,140
no peanut fields. Instead, he was dismayed to see only

128
00:08:27,141 --> 00:08:31,140
growing in the dry, sandy soil.

129
00:08:31,240 --> 00:08:36,140
In 1794, Eli Whitney invented the cotton gin.

130
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:41,780
And from that date on into 1900, cotton was king.

131
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:45,700
Carver knew that a single crop system was not good for the soil.

132
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:51,100
Any time in agriculture you base your economy on one single crop,

133
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:53,140
you're vulnerable.

134
00:08:53,141 --> 00:08:56,880
In a fertility standpoint, you're depleting the soil and maybe

135
00:08:56,980 --> 00:09:00,880
not returning all the nutrients there that is taken.

136
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:03,680
Carver also recognized that the only thing more vulnerable

137
00:09:03,780 --> 00:09:08,160
than the nutrient deficient soil was the multitude of poor black

138
00:09:08,260 --> 00:09:11,620
sharecroppers working in the cotton fields from sunrise to

139
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:14,660
sunset. Carver was anxious to get to work

140
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:18,300
to solve the South's agricultural dilemma.

141
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:21,000
But when he arrived at Tuskegee Institute, he

142
00:09:21,001 --> 00:09:23,740
He discovered that he didn't even have a laboratory in which

143
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:25,940
to work.

144
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:29,500
And Tuskegee had no money to properly establish one.

145
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:32,440
So he went out into a local junkyard

146
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:35,140
and found old pots and pans and spoons

147
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:38,080
and took his students and said, we are now collecting the

148
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:40,120
implements for our laboratory.

149
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:43,060
He was the absolute first person to bring science

150
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:45,300
to Tuskegee University.

151
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:47,400
He was one of those great visionaries

152
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,400
that, you know, when Booker T. Washington

153
00:09:49,401 --> 00:09:52,600
and shows him this campus and there is no agricultural college,

154
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:54,100
there's nothing.

155
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:57,040
You know, he had the ability to take nothing

156
00:09:57,140 --> 00:09:58,200
and make it into something.

157
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:03,640
And I think that his life was a great example of that.

158
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:06,860
Carver started reaching out to the farmers in the area,

159
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:09,760
showing them how the cotton they grew year after year

160
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:13,560
was sapping the nitrogen and other nutrients from the soil.

161
00:10:13,660 --> 00:10:16,000
He encouraged them to plant crops that would restore these

162
00:10:16,100 --> 00:10:18,500
important elements back into the land,

163
00:10:18,501 --> 00:10:22,500
Crops like sweet potatoes, cowpeas, soybeans, and peanuts.

164
00:10:22,501 --> 00:10:26,500
Carver designed and implemented what he called a movable school

165
00:10:26,501 --> 00:10:30,500
to educate the farmers about crop rotation.

166
00:10:30,501 --> 00:10:35,500
Many took his message to heart, but few acted on his advice.

167
00:10:35,501 --> 00:10:39,500
The farmers agreed, but they did not immediately heed

168
00:10:39,501 --> 00:10:45,500
because they could not get loans at the bank for any crop

169
00:10:45,501 --> 00:10:48,500
except cotton. Carver's concern for

170
00:10:48,501 --> 00:10:51,140
agriculture wasn't restricted to how cotton was bankrupting

171
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:55,140
the soil. Another threat was lurking

172
00:10:55,240 --> 00:10:59,640
menacingly on the horizon. The boll weevil, the dreaded

173
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:02,340
insect that had already decimated the cotton crops in

174
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:07,360
Texas, was making its way across the south.

175
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:10,460
And it was moving through Mississippi, Louisiana, and

176
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:14,300
headed to Alabama and Georgia at the rate of about 100 miles

177
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:17,960
per year. And he warned them.

178
00:11:17,961 --> 00:11:21,860
There was no stopping this devastating force of nature.

179
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:25,060
The weevil actually lays its eggs inside the unripe cotton

180
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:28,900
bull, and the newborns eat their way out, destroying the crop

181
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:33,400
in the process. Carver believed the South's

182
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:37,220
best defense against the bull weevil was the peanut.

183
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:39,720
But he had to convince Southern farmers that peanuts could

184
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:44,420
become the cash crop that King Cotton had been for so long.

185
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:47,360
George Washington Carver really became

186
00:11:47,361 --> 00:11:52,360
evangelist for peanut growing in the southeast part of the United

187
00:11:52,361 --> 00:11:57,360
States. In 1902, Carver entered his lab

188
00:11:57,361 --> 00:12:01,360
at Tuskegee to begin intense research into the peanut.

189
00:12:01,361 --> 00:12:04,360
He explored its properties and looked for ways to put the

190
00:12:04,361 --> 00:12:09,360
protein and vitamin-rich crop to use.

191
00:12:09,361 --> 00:12:14,360
Peanuts are made up of oils, resins, fats, sugars, and

192
00:12:14,361 --> 00:12:16,360
and starches.

193
00:12:16,361 --> 00:12:20,360
And Carver came up with more than 300 new uses for them,

194
00:12:20,361 --> 00:12:25,360
in recipes and in a variety of synthetic applications.

195
00:12:25,361 --> 00:12:31,360
He made paper and ink and shoe polish and floor wax

196
00:12:31,361 --> 00:12:35,360
and axle grease, and he did have some kind of a peanut

197
00:12:35,361 --> 00:12:38,360
plastic that he'd made. And all of these uses,

198
00:12:38,361 --> 00:12:43,360
300 of them, all the way from their soups,

199
00:12:43,361 --> 00:12:47,360
soups to soap and shaving cream.

200
00:12:47,361 --> 00:12:51,260
He built the foundation block upon which peanuts were ushered

201
00:12:51,261 --> 00:12:53,760
in.

202
00:12:53,761 --> 00:12:57,800
As Carver predicted, in 1915, the boll weevil hit the

203
00:12:57,801 --> 00:13:00,600
southeastern farms and completely destroyed the

204
00:13:00,601 --> 00:13:04,520
cotton fields. None of the farmers from Georgia or

205
00:13:04,521 --> 00:13:09,520
Alabama could pay off the loans on their land.

206
00:13:09,521 --> 00:13:13,360
But thanks to Carver's zealous advocacy, peanuts were poured

207
00:13:13,361 --> 00:13:17,860
to become a viable commercial alternative to cotton.

208
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:22,800
By 1917, cotton was dethroned and peanuts was lifted up,

209
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:29,600
and the acres increased in the southeast 400% in just two years.

210
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:32,520
As peanut power spread across the south,

211
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:37,620
Carver continued to experiment with a now lucrative crop.

212
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:39,960
While analyzing the properties of peanut oil,

213
00:13:40,060 --> 00:13:42,960
he discovered that it was very easily absorbed when rubbed

214
00:13:42,961 --> 00:13:48,460
onto the skin. Carver promoted peanut oil

215
00:13:48,461 --> 00:13:51,260
as an effective lubricant to be used in vigorous massage

216
00:13:51,261 --> 00:13:54,200
treatments for those suffering from muscular disorders.

217
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:03,200
Thousands of patients, including those crippled by polio, came to

218
00:14:03,201 --> 00:14:06,020
Tuskegee to receive peanut oil massages.

219
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:11,520
Even more wrote to Carver to purchase the oil.

220
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:16,200
considered a cure for any disease, those who underwent

221
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:18,360
the treatment reported encouraging results.

222
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:24,540
Word of Carver's promising work made it all the way to the White

223
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:26,580
House.

224
00:14:26,680 --> 00:14:29,340
One of the extractions that he did with the peanut,

225
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:31,520
one of our presidents, Roosevelt,

226
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:35,320
used it for his infantile paralysis.

227
00:14:35,420 --> 00:14:39,220
In 1942, President Franklin Delano Roosevelt paid an

228
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:42,960
official visit to Tuskegee.

229
00:14:42,961 --> 00:14:46,960
he greeted Carver in person and applauded the research being done

230
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:50,960
on the Alabama campus.

231
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:54,940
I'm proud to come to Tuskegee because I'm proud of what

232
00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:56,940
Tuskegee has done.

233
00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:02,020
While Carver has been called the father of the peanut

234
00:15:02,120 --> 00:15:05,480
industry, there is one product that many mistakenly attribute

235
00:15:05,580 --> 00:15:09,620
to him, peanut butter.

236
00:15:09,621 --> 00:15:17,620
Peanut butter as we know it in America was basically invented in 1890 by a physician in Missouri.

237
00:15:17,621 --> 00:15:26,620
And he was looking for high protein, nutritious foods to feed his patients who had trouble eating meat because they had poor dental health.

238
00:15:26,621 --> 00:15:36,107
The first real introduction to the masses for peanut butter happened in 1904 at the Universal Exposition in St.

239
00:15:36,108 --> 00:15:36,808
Louis.

240
00:15:36,809 --> 00:15:39,080
and that's when really peanut butter was introduced

241
00:15:39,081 --> 00:15:40,460
to the American public.

242
00:15:40,461 --> 00:15:43,960
He had laid the foundation for raising up

243
00:15:43,961 --> 00:15:46,120
and digging deep into the kernel

244
00:15:46,121 --> 00:15:48,160
and chemically telling the world

245
00:15:48,161 --> 00:15:52,160
what a wonderful food product peanut was.

246
00:15:52,161 --> 00:15:54,780
His love of peanuts helped to really promote

247
00:15:54,781 --> 00:15:58,080
its usage widespread, and I think that, you know,

248
00:15:58,081 --> 00:16:00,580
he would be very happy to see how his legacy

249
00:16:00,581 --> 00:16:04,080
has really taken shape in modern-day America.

250
00:16:06,620 --> 00:16:13,160
We basically take a peanut that George Washington Carver helped develop and build in the field

251
00:16:13,161 --> 00:16:19,260
and help it grow to what it is today to the point that we now buy the peanut, we shell

252
00:16:19,261 --> 00:16:23,960
