00:26:50,841 --> 00:26:53,840
Diamond Grove, Missouri, when he was a child.

410
00:26:56,840 --> 00:26:59,840
Continuing another legacy from his childhood, Carver devised a

411
00:26:59,841 --> 00:27:03,840
way to make various pigments and paint from the soil he found

412
00:27:03,841 --> 00:27:08,840
in the Alabama countryside. He combined the clay soil with

413
00:27:08,841 --> 00:27:13,840
equal amounts of sulfuric and hydrochloric acids, and the

414
00:27:13,841 --> 00:27:17,540
bits of scrap iron. Carver then boiled the mixture

415
00:27:17,541 --> 00:27:22,040
until the iron dissolved. The result was a dry paste that

416
00:27:22,041 --> 00:27:24,480
could be combined with oil to make paint.

417
00:27:24,481 --> 00:27:27,280
At that time, it was absolutely phenomenal.

418
00:27:27,281 --> 00:27:32,100
And to see the pure royal blues that he could extract from

419
00:27:32,101 --> 00:27:36,900
Alabama clay, just is mind-boggling.

